WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.404
Razor Pages applications often use
cookies, session state, TempData,

2
00:00:07.484 --> 00:00:14.733
hidden fields, query strings, and route
values to support user workflows.

3
00:00:14.813 --> 00:00:20.796
These mechanisms can make multi-step
experiences smoother,

4
00:00:20.876 --> 00:00:28.126
but they also carry assumptions about
trust, lifetime, scope, and source.

5
00:00:28.206 --> 00:00:34.879
User state deserves the same review
attention as forms and handlers.

6
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:42.669
Cookies are security-sensitive because
they may carry authentication state,

7
00:00:42.749 --> 00:00:50.343
correlation state, antiforgery state,
preferences, workflow identifiers,

8
00:00:50.423 --> 00:00:52.954
or application settings.

9
00:00:53.034 --> 00:00:59.018
Review SameSite, Secure, HttpOnly,
lifetime, path, domain,

10
00:00:59.098 --> 00:01:03.585
and purpose for security-sensitive
cookies.

11
00:01:03.665 --> 00:01:11.260
A cookie choice that is reasonable for a
preference may be wrong for a session or

12
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.526
workflow identifier.

13
00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:19.935
Sensitive data should be minimized in
client-controlled state.

14
00:01:20.015 --> 00:01:23.927
The browser can store and return values,

15
00:01:24.007 --> 00:01:28.955
but that does not make those values
authoritative.

16
00:01:29.035 --> 00:01:33.407
Hidden fields, query strings, route
values,

17
00:01:33.487 --> 00:01:41.772
and client-stored preferences should not
become trusted sources for authorization

18
00:01:41.852 --> 00:01:48.295
decisions, pricing, ownership, role,
tenant, or approval state.

19
00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:54.819
Session and TempData can be useful for
short workflows, messages,

20
00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:56.970
and temporary state.

21
00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:05.105
They should not become hidden stores for
secrets, broad authorization decisions,

22
00:02:05.185 --> 00:02:09.903
or important business state that no one
reviews.

23
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:18.037
If a handler depends on session or
TempData to make a security-sensitive decision,

24
00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:26.287
reviewers should understand why that value
is trustworthy and how it is maintained.

25
00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:34.536
Server-side authorization should be based
on trusted server-side data and current

26
00:02:34.616 --> 00:02:35.422
policy.

27
00:02:35.502 --> 00:02:40.105
A page can use user state to improve
experience,

28
00:02:40.185 --> 00:02:47.894
but the final decision should be validated
against records, identity, policy,

29
00:02:47.974 --> 00:02:52.346
and workflow state that the server
controls.

30
00:02:52.426 --> 00:03:00.596
This distinction prevents convenience
state from becoming accidental authority.

31
00:03:00.676 --> 00:03:07.350
The baseline is conservative: keep
client-controlled state small,

32
00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.227
protect cookies according to purpose,

33
00:03:11.307 --> 00:03:15.334
avoid storing secrets in workflow state,

34
00:03:15.414 --> 00:03:19.557
validate state transitions on the server,

35
00:03:19.637 --> 00:03:25.620
and make user-state assumptions visible
during page review.
